<p>This one is hard for me. Because it's like&#8230; it kind of rides that line between humor and brutality. But my issues lie largely in execution.</p>
<p>While I'm not one usually to harp much on &quot;immersion&quot;, the sudden switch from the SCP document to the tale-thing felt extremely jarring to me. Mostly because I couldn't stop asking myself:</p>
<p>&quot;Why did this have to be a tale? Why couldn't you have just continued it in the skip?&quot;</p>
<p>And while there's a few possible reasons, things like wanting to utilize the prose and the different colored dialogue, the thought &quot;but why not just put this in a transcript&quot; heavily distracted me from the actual content of the tale. And by proxy of that, I found myself hung up on how expository I felt iris was being in the end of that tale. It's not as annoying to me as most exposition because you do a good job of injecting character and voice into it, but at the end of the day I couldn't separate it from being just an explanation, with the whole time in my head asking myself &quot;but why did you have to explain it in prose?&quot;</p>
<p>All in all&#8230; it's hard for me because I wanted to enjoy this. This had a lot of things going for it from the &quot;useless parasite&quot; angle to the brutality of eating a species just to feel superior to it. But the presentation of it distracted me from the content because I couldn't stop asking myself &quot;but whyyyy?&quot;</p>
<p>I think I'm going to have to downvote. Not because I think this is terrible, but because I can't help but think this could've hit harder.</p>

							</div>

								
					
